WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.708
In this video, we present PGX: physically grounded execution for long-horizon humanoid loco-manipulation.

2
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:15.904
Humans can easily learn and perform long-horizon tasks, such as brewing and fetching a cup of coffee. Can humanoid robots do the same?

3
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:27.120
Flat end-to-end VLA methods drop the mug, or walk past the coffee machine. A state machine alone still fails at the machine. PGX, our method, completes the entire long-horizon task.

4
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:38.280
PGX separates timing, skills, and precision. Physics decides when and where to act; the VLA learns how.

5
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.640
A state machine gated by physical predicates decides when to switch stages.

6
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.784
The VLA executes each manipulation skill from a stage-wise start state.

7
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:58.432
World-coordinate locking anchors the base and contact targets in the world frame. All execution layers are training-free except VLA models.

8
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:09.368
We fine-tune state-of-the-art VLA models on loco-manipulation demonstrations, and evaluate them on the grasp, place, press, re-grasp, and deliver skills.

9
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:20.608
A flat policy uses one VLA for both locomotion and manipulation, and loses track of the task: it drops the mug, or walks to the delivery area without brewing.

10
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:36.792
Inside PGX, both backbones complete the whole task: grasp, place, press, wait, re-grasp, and deliver. Final success rises from near zero to sixty percent for π₀.₅, and from thirty-five to eighty percent for GR00T.

11
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:48.720
The same executor applies to box transport and mug placement, lifting both backbones to reliable execution and improving the average success rate.

12
00:01:51.800 --> 00:02:02.648
World-coordinate locking keeps the base at its stance. Under sustained disturbance, base drift falls from fifty-four to nine centimetres, and completed placements rise from seventy-four to ninety-seven percent.

13
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.656
Finally, we deploy PGX on a real Unitree G1 with Dex-3 hands. The VLA is fine-tuned on forty-four real demonstrations.

14
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.200
The robot places the mug at the machine, waits for brewing, then re-grasps it.

15
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.112
It carries the mug back and delivers it.

16
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.672
Thank you for watching.
